<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Johann Gottlieb Fichte</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1762">1762</a>–<a href="/years/1814">1814</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Johann Gottlieb Fichte was a German philosopher and a founding figure of German idealism, which developed from the writings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/immanuel-kant">Immanuel Kant</a>. Born in Rammenau, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-lusatia">Upper Lusatia</a>, the son of a ribbon weaver, he first won attention with Attempt at a Critique of All Revelation (1792), published without his name and at first believed to be a new work by Kant. He taught at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-jena">University of Jena</a> from 1794 until his dismissal for atheism in 1799, then moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a>. His Addresses to the German Nation (1807–1808) gave him a reputation as one of the fathers of German nationalism. He was elected rector of the new <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/humboldt-university-of-berlin">University of Berlin</a> and died of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/typhus">typhus</a> in 1814.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/johann-gottlieb-fichte.jpg" alt="Johann Gottlieb Fichte" width="330" height="377" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Johann_Gottlieb_Fichte.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Fichte was born on 19 May 1762 in Rammenau and baptised a Lutheran, into a pious family that had lived in the region for generations. He was the son of a ribbon weaver, Christian Fichte, who married Maria Dorothea Schurich somewhat above his class. Fichte attended the foundation school at Pforta from October 1774. Miltitz's widow supported him until 1784, which let him study theology at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jena">Jena</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leipzig">Leipzig</a>, but when she withdrew her help he had to leave without a degree and worked as a tutor for several families, and in Zürich met his future wife, Johanna Maria Rahn.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In the summer of 1790 Fichte began to study the works of Kant. After a disappointing interview with Kant at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konigsberg">Königsberg</a> on 4 July 1791 he wrote, in five weeks, the Attempt at a Critique of All Revelation. The first edition appeared without his name or preface and was believed to be a new work by Kant, until Kant cleared up the confusion and praised the author, and Fichte's reputation soared. He married Johanna on 22 October 1793. He was invited to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-jena">University of Jena</a> and began lecturing in May 1794, expounding his system of transcendental idealism with success.</p><p>In 1798 he was accused of atheism over an essay in which he argued that the living moral order is itself God. He was dismissed in 1799 and, since every German state but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> had joined the outcry, went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a>, where he joined the circle of Friedrich and August Wilhelm Schlegel, Schleiermacher and Schelling. In 1807 and 1808 he delivered the Addresses to the German Nation, which attempted to define the German nation. In the new <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/humboldt-university-of-berlin">University of Berlin</a>, founded in 1810, he was unanimously elected rector, but resigned in 1812 after friction. He died of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/typhus">typhus</a> on 29 January 1814, aged 51.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Fichte rejected Kant's things in themselves, the supra-sensible reality beyond perception, as an invitation to skepticism. He argued instead that consciousness is not grounded in anything outside itself and that the phenomenal world arises from consciousness, the activity of the I, and moral awareness. In Foundations of Natural Right (1797) he argued that self-consciousness is a social phenomenon: an individual becomes aware of itself as free only when other rational subjects summon it, and mutual recognition is a condition for the individual I. He is also the originator of thesis, antithesis and synthesis, an idea often attributed to Hegel in error.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Johann Gottlieb Fichte” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Immanuel Kant and the idealist philosophers who followed him, <b>Johann Gottlieb Fichte</b> and Friedrich Schelling, stressed the creative power of the mind.</blockquote><a href="/movement/romanticism">Romanticism · Background</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Rammenau, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.15,14.13&amp;year=1795" aria-label="Rammenau on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4871 391 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Johann Gottlieb Fichte"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5321 661h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5321 661h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=51.15,14.13&amp;year=1795">What was here in 1795? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
